um let's see here good evening council members my name is Charles and I'm here to address chapter 128 of the proposed ordinance um it seems like a lot of people have touched on a lot of these points but maybe they haven't here a couple of the points I got the main point I wanted to make tonight is simple though the the chapter 128 uh it violates the law specifically it violates two critical legal fa frameworks wyoming state statute and the United States Constitution particularly the Fourth Amendment um there are actually four major violations in this ordinance first it's the wrong judge the ordinance uses a municipal judge instead of a district court judge which directly violates Wyoming statute section 35-7-1046 second there's no pre-notice it allows secret warrants without notifying the property owner which violates the Supreme Court's ruling in the Patel case third no hearing it issues warrants exparte that means secretly without the owner present to argue their case fourth wrong scope it uses fire code authority for non-fire issues like weeds and

zoning violations let me walk through each of those in detail let's start with violation number one state law Wyoming statute section 35-7-1046 is crystal clear it requires that district court judge must issue these administrative warrants but city ordinance 1.28 uses a municipal judge instead a judge who is hired and fired by the city manager the result the city is lowering the standard set by state law this makes the ordinance void abinitio that's a legal term meaning invalid from the very start you cannot use a lower standard than what the state requires that's not how the law works now um let's talk about violations two and three secret searches in 2015 the Supreme Court ruled in city of Los Angeles vers Patel that administrative searches require two things pre-notice to the owner a pre-hering before a neutral judge but chapter 1.28 allows the city to skip both notice and

hearing if they claim it would frustrate the purpose of the inspection here's the reality announcing a search for weeds doesn't make the weeds disappear this exception effectively allows secret searches for every code violation that's unconstitutional the Fourth Amendment protects us from exactly this kind of government overreach violation number four misusing the fire code the Wyoming fire code title 35-9111 is limited in scope it applies only to structures that are especially liable to fire but the city is using this fire code authority for weeds not a fire issue junk not a fire issue zoning violations not a fire issue building code violations not a fire issue the result the city is exceeding the authority granted by state fire codes you can't take a narrow power and

expand it to cover everything that's not legal so in conclusion here's my request vote no on chapter 1.28 28 if you genuinely need enforcement powers then draft a legal ordinance one that uses district judges as required by state statute one that requires notice and hearing as required by the constitution one that limits enforcement to actual fire hazards as required by fire uh code scope this isn't about preventing enforcement it's about doing it legally thank you for your time thank you sir question clarification i'm sorry um thank you Mor uh thank you sir testimony could you provide the number for that uh regarding the circuit court judge versus municipal court judge i didn't write fast enough the uh

